# PGB deployment caching rules (Apache).
#
# Why this file exists: everything under assets/ is content-hashed, so a given filename's
# contents never change; index.html is not hashed, and it is the only thing that names the
# current bundle. Without explicit rules Apache sends neither Cache-Control nor Expires for
# index.html, and browsers then apply *heuristic* freshness — roughly 10% of the document's
# age — so a months-old index.html is treated as fresh for weeks and is not even
# revalidated. The tab keeps loading the old assets/index-<hash>.js it names, and the app
# silently runs an old version. That is exactly what happened at pangenome.ucsd.edu.
#
# This file only takes effect if the site's Apache config allows overrides for this
# directory (`AllowOverride FileInfo Options Indexes` or `AllowOverride All`). If it does
# not, the same directives can be pasted into the <Directory> block of the site config
# instead. See docs/agents/deployment-instructions.md.

<IfModule mod_headers.c>

    # index.html: always revalidate. `no-cache` does not mean "do not store" — the browser
    # may keep it, but must check with the server before reuse. A 304 makes that check
    # nearly free, and it guarantees a reload picks up a new deployment.
    <FilesMatch "^index\.html$">
        Header set Cache-Control "no-cache, must-revalidate"
    </FilesMatch>

    # Content-hashed build output: cache hard and never revalidate. A new build produces a
    # new filename, so there is no such thing as a stale hit here.
    <FilesMatch "-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{8,}\.(js|css|png|jpg|jpeg|svg|woff2?)$">
        Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
    </FilesMatch>

    # Datasets and registries: not hashed, and they do change between releases. An hour of
    # caching keeps the app quick without stranding a researcher on last month's registry.
    <FilesMatch "\.(json|tsv)$">
        Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate"
    </FilesMatch>

</IfModule>

# Range requests are load-bearing: the annotation tracks read BigBed and .fai indexes by
# byte range. Compressing those transparently would break range arithmetic.
<IfModule mod_deflate.c>
    AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE text/html text/css application/javascript application/json text/tab-separated-values
</IfModule>

# Nothing below this point, deliberately: every directive in this file belongs to Apache's
# `FileInfo` override class, so the file is either honoured or ignored depending on
# `AllowOverride` — it can never be fatal. A directive from another class (`Options
# -Indexes` was here, and `Options` is its own class) makes Apache reject the whole
# directory with a 500 wherever the override is not granted, which is a far worse outcome
# than the caching problem this file exists to solve. Turning off directory listing is a
# server-config recommendation in docs/DEPLOYMENT.md instead.
